# Target C-II — the canonical reflection braid thickens to four-strand antipodal stars

**Date:** 2026-09-17  
**Status:** proof-only strengthening of the `eta=3/26` slow-scale reflection branch; no canonical promotion claimed  
**Scope:** current canonical central reflection braid  
**Dependencies:** canonical farthest reflection blockers; slow-scale central reflection braid; shallow-tooth penetration matching  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_tooth_reflection_thick_star_braid_2026_09_17.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The current central braid uses only one long source-pair segment from each five-point tooth gadget, namely the reflection blocker `x_i y_i`. The other three local source points are only unit distance from `x_i`:

\[
|a_i-x_i|=|b_i-x_i|=|w_i-x_i|=1.
\]

At the slow scale this `O(1)` endpoint perturbation is negligible compared with both the `D-O(1)` blocker length and the `kappa_D D^{-1/4}` angular separation between different gadgets. Therefore the entire four-ray star

\[
\boxed{
\mathcal S_i=
\{y_i a_i,\ y_i b_i,\ y_i x_i,\ y_i w_i\}
}
\tag{SB.1}
\]

inherits the crossing braid.

For every

\[
\kappa_D\to\infty,
\qquad
\kappa_D=o(D^{1/4}),
\]

there are more than

\[
\boxed{
(0.129220199818177\ldots-o(1))
{D^{1/4}\over\kappa_D}
}
\tag{SB.2}

pairwise-disjoint five-point gadgets such that:

1. every segment in every star has length greater than
   \[
   D-\left(1+{\sqrt{303}\over26}\right)
   =D-1.669495968674200\ldots;
   \]
2. the four rays in one star have the common exposed hull head `y_i`;
3. if `i!=j`, **every one of the sixteen cross-star segment pairs**
   \[
   y_i z_i,\qquad y_j z_j,
   \qquad
   z_i\in\{a_i,b_i,x_i,w_i\},
   \quad
   z_j\in\{a_j,b_j,x_j,w_j\}
   \]
   crosses properly;
4. every such proper crossing lies in one common
   \[
   O(D/\kappa_D)=o(D)
   \]
   disk about the source circumcenter.

Thus every two distinct stars form a complete `4 by 4` crossing system.  No assertion is needed about intersections among rays belonging to the same star beyond their common head.

The penetration contact also acquires a fixed support-depth location relative to the canonical hull normal. If `N_i` is the outward support normal at `y_i`, then

\[
\boxed{
\operatorname{depth}_{N_i}(w_i)
>{\sqrt3\over2}-o(1),
}
\tag{SB.3}

while the shallow apex obeys

\[
\boxed{
\operatorname{depth}_{N_i}(x_i)
<{\sqrt{303}\over26}
=0.669495968674200\ldots .
}
\tag{SB.4}

Consequently the actual penetration contact crosses a much larger fixed normal-depth gap:

\[
\boxed{
\operatorname{depth}_{N_i}(w_i)
-
\operatorname{depth}_{N_i}(x_i)
>
{49\sqrt3-\sqrt{303}\over104}-o(1)
=0.648688407551402\ldots-o(1).
}
\tag{SB.5}
\]
The loaded base endpoints lie even shallower, below depth `0.630872`. The deep penetration endpoint remains within a fixed opposite-hull layer,

\[
\boxed{
{\sqrt3\over2}-o(1)
<\operatorname{depth}_{N_i}(w_i)
<1+{\sqrt{303}\over26}
=1.669495968674200\ldots .
}
\tag{SB.6}

This couples the object that the preceding reflection work had left separate: the ordered near-diametral central braid and the actual inward unit penetration contacts now belong to one thick geometric braid, with an explicit support-depth jump at every tooth.

---

## 1. Start from the canonical central braid

Use the canonical farthest-blocker refinement at

\[
\eta_*={3\over26}.
\]

For each retained gadget write

\[
G_i=\{a_i,b_i,x_i,w_i,y_i\},
\]

where

- `a_i,b_i` are the loaded base endpoints;
- `x_i` is the shallow apex;
- `w_i` is the chosen inward penetration endpoint;
- `y_i` is the canonical farthest reflection blocker head.

The five-point sets `G_i` are pairwise disjoint.

Put

\[
C_*={\sqrt{303}\over26}=0.669495968674200\ldots .
\tag{SB.7}
\]

The blocker segment

\[
e_i=x_i y_i
\]

satisfies

\[
|e_i|>D-C_*.
\tag{SB.8}
\]

Its midpoint `m_i` obeys

\[
|m_i-o|=O(D^{3/4}),
\tag{SB.9}
\]

and for distinct selected gadgets the supporting-line angle satisfies

\[
\phi_{ij}
\ge(2-o(1))\kappa_DD^{-1/4}.
\tag{SB.10}
\]

The selected population is (SB.2), and all blocker intersections already lie in `B(o,O(D/kappa_D))`.

---

## 2. Every local tail point gives another fixed-slack long segment

Define the four-point tail cluster

\[
T_i:=\{a_i,b_i,x_i,w_i\}.
\]

Every `z in T_i` satisfies

\[
|z-x_i|\le1.
\tag{SB.11}
\]

Indeed the inequality is equality for `a_i,b_i,w_i`, while it is zero for `x_i`.

Therefore, by (SB.8),

\[
\begin{aligned}
|y_i-z|
&\ge |y_i-x_i|-|z-x_i|\\
&>D-C_*-1.
\end{aligned}
\]

Hence

\[
\boxed{
|y_i-z|
>D-C_{\rm thick},
\qquad
C_{\rm thick}:=1+C_*
=1.669495968674200\ldots .
}
\tag{SB.12}
\]

This estimate applies simultaneously to all four rays of every star.

---

## 3. Thickening changes directions by only `O(D^-1)`

Orient the original blocker from its tail to its head:

\[
d_i={y_i-x_i\over|y_i-x_i|}.
\]

For `z in T_i`, put

\[
d_{i,z}={y_i-z\over|y_i-z|}.
\]

The two unnormalized vectors differ by `z-x_i`, of norm at most one. Both lengths exceed `D-C_thick`. The normalized-vector estimate gives

\[
\boxed{
|d_{i,z}-d_i|
\le {2\over D-C_{\rm thick}}
=O(D^{-1}).
}
\tag{SB.13}
\]

Thus

\[
\angle(d_{i,z},d_i)=O(D^{-1}).
\tag{SB.14}
\]

Since

\[
{D^{-1}\over\kappa_DD^{-1/4}}
={1\over\kappa_DD^{3/4}}
\to0,
\]

the thickened rays from distinct gadgets retain the same angular separation:

\[
\boxed{
\angle(d_{i,z_i},d_{j,z_j})
\ge(2-o(1))\kappa_DD^{-1/4}
}
\tag{SB.15}
\]

uniformly over all sixteen choices of `z_i,z_j`.

---

## 4. Every cross-star ray pair intersects in the common central core

Let

\[
m_{i,z}:={y_i+z\over2}
\]

be the midpoint of one thickened ray. From (SB.11),

\[
|m_{i,z}-m_i|
\le{1\over2}.
\tag{SB.16}
\]

Together with (SB.9),

\[
|m_{i,z}-o|=O(D^{3/4})
\tag{SB.17}
\]

uniformly over all selected gadgets and all four tail choices.

Take distinct gadgets `i,j` and arbitrary `z_i in T_i`, `z_j in T_j`. Let `q` be the intersection of the two supporting lines. The standard two-line estimate and (SB.15)--(SB.17) give

\[
\begin{aligned}
|q-m_{i,z_i}|,
|q-m_{j,z_j}|
&\le
{O(D^{3/4})\over
 \sin((2-o(1))\kappa_DD^{-1/4})}\\
&=O(D/\kappa_D).
\end{aligned}
\tag{SB.18}
\]

Because `kappa_D=o(D^{1/4})`, the `O(D^{3/4})` midpoint radius is absorbed by this scale, so

\[
\boxed{|q-o|=O(D/\kappa_D)=o(D).}
\tag{SB.19}
\]

Every thickened segment has half-length

\[
>{D-C_{\rm thick}\over2}
=\left({1\over2}-o(1)\right)D.
\]

Equation (SB.18) is `o(D)`, so `q` lies strictly inside both finite segments for sufficiently large `D`. The gadgets are vertex-disjoint, so this is a proper crossing.

Since the tail choices were arbitrary, every one of the sixteen ray pairs between two distinct stars crosses properly. Within one star all four rays share `y_i`. This proves the complete cross-star crossing statement.

---

## 5. The penetration endpoint sits in a fixed deeper support layer

Let `N_i` be the canonical outward hull normal at `y_i`, and let `n_i` be the inward loaded-base normal. The canonical slow-scale theorem gives

\[
\angle(N_i,n_i)=O(D^{-1/4}).
\tag{SB.20}
\]

Let

\[
u_i:=w_i-x_i,
\qquad |u_i|=1.
\]

The penetration theorem gives

\[
\boxed{
u_i\cdot n_i
>\sin(\pi/3-\alpha_i).
}
\tag{SB.21}
\]

Therefore

\[
u_i\cdot N_i
>\sin(\pi/3-\alpha_i)-o(1).
\tag{SB.22}
\]

The canonical support-cap proof also gives a lower bound for the apex depth. With `theta_i=angle(N_i,n_i)`, the base-midpoint inequality is

\[
s_{p,i}\ge \cos\alpha_i\sin\theta_i.
\]

Thus

\[
\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{depth}_{N_i}(x_i)
&=s_{p,i}+\sin\alpha_i\cos\theta_i\\
&\ge
\cos\alpha_i\sin\theta_i
+\sin\alpha_i\cos\theta_i\\
&=\sin(\alpha_i+\theta_i)\\
&\ge\sin\alpha_i.
\end{aligned}
\tag{SB.23}
\]

Adding (SB.22),

\[
\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{depth}_{N_i}(w_i)
&=
\operatorname{depth}_{N_i}(x_i)
+u_i\cdot N_i\\
&>
\sin\alpha_i
+\sin(\pi/3-\alpha_i)
-o(1)\\
&=
\cos(\alpha_i-\pi/6)-o(1).
\end{aligned}
\tag{SB.24}
\]

For

\[
0\le\alpha_i<\pi/6,
\]

one has

\[
\cos(\alpha_i-\pi/6)\ge\cos(\pi/6)={\sqrt3\over2}.
\]

Therefore

\[
\boxed{
\operatorname{depth}_{N_i}(w_i)
>{\sqrt3\over2}-o(1),
}
\tag{SB.25}
\]

which is (SB.3).

---

## 6. The penetration edge has a fixed normal component

Equation (SB.22) already gives the exact depth jump, because

\[
\operatorname{depth}_{N_i}(w_i)
-\operatorname{depth}_{N_i}(x_i)
=u_i\cdot N_i.
\]

For `0<=alpha_i<=alpha_*`, the function `sin(pi/3-alpha_i)` decreases, so

\[
\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{depth}_{N_i}(w_i)-\operatorname{depth}_{N_i}(x_i)
&>\sin(\pi/3-\alpha_*)-o(1)\\
&={49\sqrt3-\sqrt{303}\over104}-o(1)\\
&=0.648688407551402\ldots-o(1).
\end{aligned}
\tag{SB.26}
\]

This proves (SB.5). The weaker absolute layer separation in (SB.3)--(SB.4) is useful because it locates both endpoints relative to the actual opposite support line, but the contact itself has the substantially larger normal jump (SB.26).

The upper depth bound is immediate from the unit contact:

\[
\operatorname{depth}_{N_i}(w_i)
\le
\operatorname{depth}_{N_i}(x_i)+1
<1+C_*.
\tag{SB.27}
\]

Together with (SB.25), this proves (SB.6).

The loaded base endpoints satisfy the sharper canonical bound

\[
\operatorname{depth}_{N_i}(a_i),
\operatorname{depth}_{N_i}(b_i)
<0.6308712013\ldots,
\]

so the penetration endpoint lies a fixed normal distance deeper than the entire three-point shallow tooth cap.

---

## 7. Frontier effect

The slow-scale reflection survivor now has a thick source-level form.

For

\[
M_D>
(0.129220199818177\ldots-o(1)){D^{1/4}\over\kappa_D},
\]

there are `M_D` pairwise-disjoint gadgets satisfying simultaneously:

```text
one exposed source-hull head y_i;
one shallow loaded base a_i b_i;
one shallow apex x_i;
one actual inward penetration contact x_i w_i;

four D-O(1) source-pair rays
    y_i a_i, y_i b_i, y_i x_i, y_i w_i;
all four rays are O(D^-1)-parallel to the same canonical hull normal;

between distinct gadgets every ray crosses every ray properly;
all these crossings lie in one O(D/kappa_D)=o(D) central core;

a_i,b_i depth < 0.630872;
x_i depth < 0.669496;
w_i depth > 0.866025-o(1).
```

Thus the penetration contacts are no longer merely labels attached to the reflection braid. They produce a second long strand `y_iw_i` in every gadget, and in fact the two loaded base endpoints give two more. Across distinct gadgets these four-strand stars form a complete crossing system through the same sublinear central core.

This still does not close C-II: a near-circular source can geometrically accommodate such radial star bundles. The next step must use that three of the four tail relations are **actual unit contacts** while all four corresponding long pairs are simultaneously near diametral. A useful target is an exact multi-point diameter/contact variation for one star, then a simultaneous variation across the ordered support-normal family.

**Disposition:** thickened every slow-scale reflection blocker to a four-ray antipodal star, proved complete cross-star crossing in the same `o(D)` core, and placed the penetration endpoint in a fixed deeper support layer with penetration-edge normal jump `0.648688...-o(1)`. C-II and Level I remain open.
